Z||000000||Eddie||10-07-1999||06:19 PM||||OK <BR>	Today I was on the bus to work and I was talking with gearhead. I TOLD him that my exhaust header was truning red from alose plug wire he said that is because the fuel was burning in the pipe.HE also said he has 2.5 in his grand am and told me when he got the car 2nd hand  that the wires were not in the right firing order the car ran  but the idle was a little off still had power!!(now here it comes) ON DIS motors the  plug that fires on the power stroke gets must off the ignition power the plug on the exhaust only gets little power from the ignition. SO if the wires are not in the right FIRING order the ignition will not burn all the gas. This HE said let alot of the gas on the power stroke go riht down the pipe and burn. And that is what made his manifold trun red <BR>	MY ? IS COULD THAT BE TRUE||||reg||5
Z||000001||MIKLAV||10-07-1999||08:48 PM||||My brother's Cavalier did the same thing.  His exhuast manifold would turn cheery red and engine would lack power.  A friend tore out the catalytic converter and the engine ran GREAT! No more cheery red exhuast manifold.  See if the converter is working properly.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-07-1999||09:45 PM||||If we're talking DIS motors.... the firing order is semi irrelavant. As long as the pairs of plugs go to the right coil, it doesn't really mater what terminal they are on. The terminals are common with the end of the coil winding. The law of physics decide where the bulk of the energy goes.||||reg||1
